<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Modeling with Differential Equations</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose you have a tank that holds 100 liters
        of pure water.  At some point, you turn on a
        faucet that causes salt water to flow into
        the tank.  Three liters flow in every minute
        and there are 20 grams of salt in every
        liter.  At the same time, you begin to drain
        water out of the tank at the rate of 3 liters
        per minute.
      </p>
      <p>
        We will find a differential equation for
        <m>S(t)</m>, the number of grams of salt in
        the tank as a function of time.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Important:</alert> To set up the equation,
        think in terms of rates!
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How many grams of salt enter the tank every
          minute?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="32">
        <p>
          Three liters come in every minute and each liter contains 20
          grams of salt so
          <m>3 \cdot 20 = 60</m> grams per minute.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let's figure out how much salt flows out
          of the tank.  Remember that <m>S</m>
          measures the amount of salt in the tank.
        </p>
        <p>
          If there are <m>S</m> grams of salt in the
          tank, what is the concentration of salt?
          That is, how many grams are in each liter?
          (Assume that the water is well mixed.)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="32">
        <p>
          Since there are <m>S</m> grams in 100 liters, there are
          <m>S/100</m> grams per liter.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If three liters of solution are drained out
          every minute, at what rate is salt leaving
          the tank?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="32">
        <p>
          Three liters go out and each liter contains
          <m>S/100</m> grams of salt so
          <m>3\frac{S}{100}=\frac{3}{100}S</m> grams per minute.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The rate of change of the amount of salt in
          the tank is the difference of the rate at
          which salt enters and the rate at which
          salt leaves.  Write this differential
          equation below.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="32">
        <p>
          <md>
            \frac{dS}{dt} = 60 - \frac{3}{100}S
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch a graph of <m>dS/dt</m> as a
          function of <m>S</m> and use it to give a
          rough sketch of the slope field.  On the
          slope field, sketch the solution that
          corresponds to the initial value
          <m>S(0) = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="32">
        <image width="40%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/32-graph.xml"/>
        </image>
        <image width="40%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/32-slope.xml"/>
        </image>
        <p>
          Setting <m>dS/dt = 0</m> gives the stable equilibrium
          solution <m>S = 2000</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>S(t)</m> if there is initially no
          salt in the tank.  That is, find the
          solution of the differential equation for
          which <m>S(0) = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="32">
        <p>
          Rewriting the equation as
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dS}{dt} \amp = -\frac{3}{100}(S-2000)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \int\frac{1}{S-2000}~dS \amp = \int-\frac{3}{100}~dt
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \ln|S-2000| \amp = -\frac{3}{100}t+C
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              S \amp = 2000 + Ae^{-3t/100}
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          Since
          <m>S(0) = 0</m>, we see that <m>A = -2000</m>, so
          <md>
            S(t) = 2000\!\left(1 - e^{-3t/100}\right)
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch your solution below.  What happens
          to the amount of salt in the tank after a
          very long time?  Does this agree with what
          you found using the slope field?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="32">
        <p>
          As <m>t\to\infty</m>,
          <m>S\to 2000</m> grams.  The graph matches the one found
          from the slope
          field.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        When a baby is small, she grows rapidly.
        However, as she grows, the rate of growth
        slows down.  A simple model is that the
        rate of change in the baby's weight is
        <em>inversely</em> proportional to the
        baby's weight, which means that the rate
        of change is proportional to the reciprocal
        of the weight.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If we let <m>w(t)</m> be
          the baby's weight as a function of
          <m>t</m>, write a differential equation
          that expresses this assumption.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="32">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dw}{dt} = \frac{k}{w}</m> for
          some positive constant.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume that at birth the baby weighs
          8 pounds and one month later she weighs
          10 pounds.  That is, <m>w(0) = 8</m> and
          <m>w(1) = 10</m>.  Solve the differential
          equation you found above and sketch the
          solution.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="32">
        <p>
          Separating variables gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \int w~dw \amp = \int k~dt
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \frac12 w^2 \amp = kt + C
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              w^2 = \amp = 2kt + C
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          Using
          <m>w(0) = 8</m>, we see that <m>C = 64</m>.  Using
          <m>w(1) = 10</m>, we have <m>100 = 2k + 64</m>,
          so <m>2k = 36</m>.  Thus
          <md>
            w(t) = \sqrt{36t + 64}
          </md>.
        </p>
        <image width="40%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/32-graph-2.xml"/>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
